## Migration Analysis

Your code has **4 patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `ProcessLoop` while loop | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | catch block with `RollbackCurrentTransaction` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Prevents rethrow in catch | Remove — cooperative cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow |
| Blocking call interruption | `GetNextItem()` may block | Pass `CancellationToken` through the call chain |

### Why `Thread.Abort` fails on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime — it's not just deprecated, it's **completely removed**. The runtime no longer supports injecting exceptions into arbitrary thread locations.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop; no ResetAbort needed
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key changes explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — signals the token; the loop checks it cooperatively.

2. **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — the `when` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not someone else's.

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** — cooperative cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow, so there's nothing to reset. Just `break` out of the loop.

4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(2s)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of hoping it finishes during an arbitrary sleep.

5. **`GetNextItem(token)`** — you **must** pass the token into blocking calls. If `GetNextItem` blocks on a `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `collection.Take(token)`. If it blocks on a `WaitHandle`, replace with `WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { handle, token.WaitHandle })`.

---

### ⚠️ Critical pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | If it blocks indefinitely, `Cancel()` won't wake it — `Stop()` will hang at `Join()`. This is the #1 migration mistake. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers and callbacks. Always `Dispose()` it. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Without the `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter, you'll swallow unrelated cancellations. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only takes effect at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` does long work, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at intervals inside it. |
